{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790966791,"nonce":"r5Q52nlhXaPs4Uq8mqB14GzCvkE=","title":"@flow elements render past scroll containers, overlay clips, and window edges —","message":"`@flow`-attached elements (anything with `flow: name` in CSS, including the new `.blinc-press-spread`) render past:\n- Scroll container viewports (the flow keeps painting in the area scrolled out of view)\n- Overlay layer clips\n- Window edges (clamped to the render target only)\n\nRoot cause: `renderer.rs::render_flow` sets the scissor rect to `clamp(element_bounds, render_target_bounds)`. It does NOT intersect with the active clip stack (scroll clip, overlay clip, etc.) the way the SDF + image + text pipelines do via their `clip_stack` integration.\n\nSymptoms:\n- A button with `.class(\"blinc-press-spread\")` inside a scrollable list shows the press feedback bleeding above/below the visible viewport while scrolled.\n- A `flow:` element in an overlay panel shows the flow output past the overlay's clipped boundary.\n\n**Why:** `render_flow` was added as a one-shot quad render with a scissor clamp to render target bounds. The full clip-stack integration that the rest of the paint pipeline carries (clip_stack push/pop, scissor intersection with active clips) wasn't ported.\n\n**How to apply:** When wiring `@flow`-using widgets into containers that can scroll-clip them (scroll containers, overlays, panes), expect the flow rendering to leak past the clip boundary. The fix lives in `blinc_gpu::renderer::render_flow` — it needs to read the active clip rect / clip stack from the paint context and intersect that with the element-bounds viewport before setting the scissor.","files":null}]}